concepts.statement:
A function is a named, parameterized block of code you define once and invoke repeatedly:
```js
function plus1(x) {        // Define a function named "plus1"
    return x + 1;          // Return a value one larger
}
plus1(y)                   // => 4: y is 3, so 3+1

let square = function(x) { // Functions are values, too:
    return x * x;          // assign one to a variable
};                         // (semicolon ends the assignment)
square(plus1(y))           // => 16: two calls in one expression
```
ES6 adds **arrow function** syntax — `const plus1 = x => x + 1;` and `const square = x => x * x;` — a concise form used mostly for unnamed functions passed as arguments. When a function is assigned to an object property it becomes a **method**, and inside it the `this` keyword refers to the object:
```js
let a = [];
a.push(1,2,3);             // push(): a method of arrays
a.reverse();               // reverse(): another method
points.dist = function() { // Define our own method on points
    let p1 = this[0], p2 = this[1];
    let a = p2.x-p1.x, b = p2.y-p1.y;
    return Math.sqrt(a*a + b*b);  // Pythagorean theorem
};
points.dist()              // => Math.sqrt(2)
```

JavaScript supports object-oriented programming, but differently from classical languages. The ES6 `class` keyword defines a constructor plus methods; `new` creates instances:
```js
class Point {                          // Class names are capitalized.
    constructor(x, y) {                // Initialize new instances.
        this.x = x;                    // this = the new object
        this.y = y;
    }
    distance() {                      // A method of the class
        return Math.sqrt(              // Square root of x²+y²
            this.x * this.x +
            this.y * this.y);
    }
}
let p = new Point(1, 1);   // The geometric point (1,1).
p.distance()               // => Math.SQRT2
```

