concepts.statement:
The first class extends the built-in `Map` so that `get()` returns a caller-chosen default instead of `undefined` when the key is missing:
```js
class DefaultMap extends Map {
    constructor(defaultValue) {
        super();                    // Invoke superclass constructor
        this.defaultValue = defaultValue; // Remember the default
    }
    get(key) {
        if (this.has(key)) {        // If the key is already in the map,
            return super.get(key);  // return its value from superclass.
        } else {
            return this.defaultValue; // Otherwise return the default.
        }
    }
}
```
`extends` creates a subclass; `super()` calls the superclass constructor; `super.get(key)` calls the overridden method. Chapter 9 covers subclassing in depth.

The second class owns the state (a letter-count map and a total) and two behaviors — record text, render itself:
```js
class Histogram {
    constructor() {
        this.letterCounts = new DefaultMap(0); // Map from letters to counts
        this.totalLetters = 0;                 // How many letters in all
    }
    add(text) {
        // Remove whitespace, then convert to upper case
        text = text.replace(/\s/g, "").toUpperCase();
        // Count the letters, one at a time
        for(let character of text) {
            let count = this.letterCounts.get(character);
            this.letterCounts.set(character, count + 1);
            this.totalLetters++;
        }
    }
    toString() {
        let entries = [...this.letterCounts]; // Convert to array of [k,v]
        // Sort by count, then alphabetically
        entries.sort((a,b) => a[1] === b[1] ? (a[0] < b[0] ? -1 : 1) : b[1] - a[1]);
        // Convert counts to percentages
        for(let entry of entries) {
            entry[1] = entry[1] / this.totalLetters * 100;
        }
        // Format and return the display
        let lines = entries.map(
            ([l,n]) => `${l}: ${"#".repeat(Math.round(n))} ${n.toFixed(2)}%`
        );
        return lines.join("\n");
    }
}
```

Input arrives as an asynchronous stream, so the program is written as an `async` function using `for await` to consume chunks as they arrive, plus one line that ties it all together:
```js
// This async function returns a Promise that resolves
// when the histogram is complete.
async function histogramFromStdin() {
    process.stdin.setEncoding("utf-8"); // Read strings, not bytes
    let histogram = new Histogram();
    for await (let chunk of process.stdin) {
        histogram.add(chunk);
    }
    return histogram;
}

// This one line is the main program:
histogramFromStdin().then(histogram => {
    console.log(histogram.toString());
});
```

