concepts.statement:
A **boolean** value represents truth or falsehood, on or off, yes or no — only two possible values, written with the reserved words `true` and `false`. Booleans are generally the result of comparisons:
```js
a === 4   // true if a equals 4; false otherwise
```
and they drive the control structures:
```js
if (a === 4) {
    b = b + 1;
} else {
    a = a + 1;
}
```
The three boolean operators — `&&` (AND), `||` (OR), `!` (NOT) — combine and invert them: `if ((x === 0 && y === 0) || !(z === 0))` means "x and y are both zero, or z is nonzero" (Chapter 4 gives their full, value-returning semantics).

In practice, symbols serve as a **language extension mechanism**. When ES6 introduced the `for/of` loop and iterable objects, it needed to define a standard way for objects to expose their iterator — without adding a string property name that might clash. The answer: `Symbol.iterator`, a symbol stored as a property of the Symbol function object, used as the method name objects implement to be iterable. Related well-known symbols include `Symbol.hasInstance` (customizes instanceof) and others covered in Chapter 14.
```js
// A tiny iterable class using a Symbol-keyed method (Chapter 12 detail):
class Range {
    constructor(from, to) { this.from = from; this.to = to; }
    [Symbol.iterator]() { /* returns an iterator */ }
}
```
The bracket syntax `[Symbol.iterator]` — a computed property name — is how symbol-keyed methods are written.

steps.text:
```js
const A = Symbol("version");
const B = Symbol("version");
shared[A] = "A-1.0";
shared[B] = "B-2.0";
shared[A]   // => "A-1.0"
shared[B]   // => "B-2.0": both coexist
```
Both symbols have the description "version", but they are **different values** — different properties. No overwriting is possible, because no second party can ever obtain the same symbol by accident.

