concepts.statement:
Variables are declared with `let`; constants with `const`. Initialize when you declare, when possible:
```js
let i;
let sum;
let i, sum;                          // multiple names in one statement
let message = "hello";
let i = 0, j = 0, k = 0;
let x = 2, y = x*x;                  // initializers can use earlier names
```
If you omit the initializer, the variable’s value is `undefined` until assigned. `const` works like `let` except you **must** initialize immediately, and the value can never be reassigned (any attempt throws a TypeError):
```js
const H0 = 74;          // Hubble constant (km/s/Mpc)
const C = 299792.458;   // Speed of light in a vacuum (km/s)
const AU = 1.496E8;     // Astronomical Unit (km)
```
All-capitals names like H0 or HTTP_NOT_FOUND are a common (not universal) convention for constants.

Variables and constants declared with `let` and `const` are **block-scoped**: defined only within the block (curly-braced region) containing the declaration. Function bodies, class bodies, if/else clauses, and loop bodies are all blocks. A declaration at the top level, outside any block, is a **global** with global scope. Loop variables declared in a `for`/`for in`/`for of` header have the loop body as their scope:
```js
for(let i = 0, len = data.length; i < len; i++) console.log(data[i]);
for(let datum of data) console.log(datum);
for(const property in object) console.log(property);
```
You can even declare loop "variables" with `const` — legal as long as the body does not reassign the value; the const simply holds one iteration’s value at a time.

Before ES6, `var` was the only declaration. It differs from `let` in four ways:
- **No block scope**: var variables are scoped to the body of the containing **function**, no matter how deeply nested in blocks.
- **var globals become properties of the global object** (so `var x = 2;` outside a function behaves like `globalThis.x = 2;`, though not perfectly: such properties cannot be deleted). let/const globals are not properties of the global object.
- **Redeclaration is legal**: because var variables have function scope, `for(var i = 0; ...)` loops routinely redeclare the same i.
- **Hoisting**: the declaration is lifted to the top of the enclosing function — the variable can be used anywhere in the function without error; before the initialization line runs, its value is `undefined`.
```js
// The classic hoisting surprise:
var y = "global";
function f() {
    console.log(y);   // undefined — NOT "global": the hoisted
    var y = "local";  // declaration already exists up here
}
```

Outside strict mode, assigning to a name never declared with let, const or var **creates a new global variable** — no matter how deeply nested the code:
```js
function f() {
    oops = 42;   // no declaration: accidental global!
}
f();
oops            // => 42: now visible everywhere
```
This is bug-prone and one of the best arguments for strict mode, where the same code throws a ReferenceError instead. Accidentally-created globals are like var globals — properties of the global object — but unlike proper var declarations, they CAN be deleted with the delete operator.

steps.text:
```js
function f() {
    console.log(x);   // prints: undefined
    var x = 1;
    console.log(x);   // prints: 1
}
``` Hoisting moved `var x;` to the top of f — so the first log reads a declared-but-uninitialized variable: undefined. No error; just quiet wrongness.

```js
function g() {
    console.log(x);   // !ReferenceError: Cannot access 'x'
    let x = 1;        //    before initialization
}
``` The engine knows about x from the start of the block (it is hoisted), but the binding is in the **temporal dead zone** until the let statement runs. Reading it there throws.

```js
let x = "outer";
{
    let x = x + 1;   // !ReferenceError: the inner x's TDZ
}
``` The new block’s x shadows the outer one **from the top of the block** — the initializer reads the inner x, which does not exist yet. Shadowing plus TDZ is a real trap: rename the inner variable.

```js
let [x, y] = [1];
``` x gets 1; there is no second value, so y gets **undefined**. Extra values on the right are simply ignored: `[x, y] = [1, 2, 3]` leaves x==1, y==2.

```js
[, x, , y] = [1, 2, 3, 4];
``` The holes skip positions 0 and 2: x == 2 (position 1), y == 4 (position 3).

```js
[x, ...y] = [1, 2, 3, 4];
``` x == 1; the `...` before y collects **all remaining values into a new array**: y == [2, 3, 4]. (The same three dots gather function arguments in Chapter 8 — one syntax, two roles.)

```js
[x, y] = [y, x];
``` The right side is evaluated completely FIRST — building a new two-element array from the current values — then destructured into the left. That is why the swap works: both old values are captured before either assignment lands.

```js
let [first, ...rest] = "Hello";
``` first == "H"; rest == ["e","l","l","o"]. Array destructuring accepts any iterable, and strings iterate by codepoint.

```js
let points2 = { p1: [x1, y1], p2: [x2, y2] };
// points2 deep-equals points
```
After a destructuring, the left side works as a **valid literal** elsewhere. If your flipped literal reproduces the original structure, the destructuring was written correctly — the chapter’s own verification trick.

