concepts.statement:
The **object initializer** (often called an object literal) is a comma-separated list of `property: value` pairs inside braces, spanning as many lines as you like:
```js
const book = {
  title: "JavaScript: The Definitive Guide",  // property: value
  edition: 7,                                 // number value
  authors: ["Flanagan"],                       // nested initializer
  summary() {                                  // ES6 method shorthand
    return this.title + ", " + this.edition + "th ed.";
  },
};
```
A trailing comma is legal, keys may be written without quotes when they are valid identifiers, and the value can be any expression.

Four ES6+ extensions make object initializers do the work of small programs:
```js
const x = 2, y = 3;
const point = { x, y };                 // shorthand: { x: x, y: y }
const key = "dyn";
const o = { [key + "amic"]: true };     // computed key: "dynamic"
const defaults = { color: "teal", size: 1 };
const badge = { ...defaults, id: 7 };    // spread copies properties in
const rect = {
  w: 3, h: 4,
  get area() { return this.w * this.h; } // accessor property
};
```
**Shorthand** abbreviates `{ x: x }` as `{ x }`; a **computed key** `[expr]` evaluates its bracketed expression at object-creation time; **spread** `...obj` copies another object’s own enumerable string-keyed properties (later keys win); **method shorthand** omits the `function` keyword.

