concepts.statement:
**Precedence** decides how strongly an operator binds its operands when different operators mix. Property access, calls and `new` bind tightest; the comma operator is loosest. The full order this book uses, highest first:
```js
// Precedence table — highest binds tightest; L/R = associativity
//  1   ()  .  []  ?.  new (with argument list)      access, call      L
//  2   new (no argument list)                                        R
//  3   x++   x--                       postfix                       L
//  4   ++x  --x  +x  -x  ~  !  typeof void delete await                R
//  5   **                                                              R
//  6   *   /   %                                                       L
//  7   +   -                                                           L
//  8   <<  >>  >>>                                                     L
//  9   <  <=  >  >=  in  instanceof                                    L
// 10   ==  !=  ===  !==                                                L
// 11   &                                                              L
// 12   ^                                                              L
// 13   |                                                              L
// 14   &&                                                             L
// 15   ||                                                             L
// 16   ??                                                              R
// 17   ?: (conditional)                                               R
// 18   =  +=  -=  *=  /=  %=  **=  <<=  >>=  >>>=  &=  |=  ^=         R
// 19   , (comma)                                                      L
```

Precedence and associativity decide how an expression is **grouped**; which subexpression actually runs first is a separate rule — always **left to right, at every level**. In `f() + g() * h()` the multiplication binds tighter, but `f()` still executes first:
```js
let order = [];
function f() { order.push("f"); return 1; }
function g() { order.push("g"); return 2; }
function h() { order.push("h"); return 3; }
f() + g() * h();    // => 7
order               // => ["f", "g", "h"] — f ran first
```
The rule matters exactly when side effects interact; with pure operands it is invisible.

