concepts.statement:
`debugger;` is a breakpoint that lives in the source: **if a debugger program is attached, execution pauses there; if not, the statement does nothing at all**:
```js
function suspicious(o) {
  if (o === undefined) debugger;   // pause here, devtools open
  return process(o);
}
```
It was designed for era-of-console debugging; the modern practice is setting breakpoints in the browser or IDE — but a debugger line left in checked-in code is harmless (and occasionally useful for a colleague).

Strict mode is enabled by the **directive prologue** — a string literal statement at the very top of a script or function body:
```js
"use strict";            // everything below is strict

function onlyInside() {
  "use strict";         // this function only
  // ...
}
```
It must be the first statement (a directive buried after code is just an inert string expression). ES6 modules and the bodies of ES6 classes are **always strict** — no directive needed — so most modern code is strict without ever writing the line.

steps.text:
```js
function Counter() {
  this.count = 0;
}
Counter.prototype.bump = function () {
  this.count++;
  return this.count;
};
const c = new Counter();
c.bump();                    // (1) same in both modes
const loose = c.bump;
loose();                     // (2) sloppy: ?   (3) strict: ?
try { loose(); } catch (e) { log(e.name); }
```

```js
// each line: sloppy result -> strict result
undeclared = 42;        // global created -> ReferenceError
let n = 010;            // 8 -> SyntaxError (write 0o10)
function dup(a, a) {}   // allowed -> SyntaxError
function selfRef() {
  return arguments.callee;  // the function -> TypeError
}
const o = Object.freeze({ x: 1 });
o.x = 2;                // silently ignored -> TypeError
delete o.x;             // false -> TypeError
```

concepts.detail:
```
let book = {
  title: "JavaScript: The Definitive Guide",
  edition: 7,
  authors: ["David Flanagan"]
};
```
The names here are `"title"`, `"edition"` and `"authors"`; the values are a string, a number and an array. Because the collection is unordered, `book.title` and `book.edition` have no positional relationship to each other.

steps.text:
