concepts.detail:
```
for (let p in { a: 1, b: 2 }) {
  console.log(p); // "a", then "b"
}
```
Because inherited properties can appear, code that wants only own data usually pairs the loop with `hasOwnProperty` — or just uses `Object.keys`.

steps.text:
```
let proto = { inherited: 10 };
let o = Object.create(proto);
o.own = 20;
```
`o` has one own property (`own: 20`) and reaches one inherited enumerable property (`inherited: 10`).

concepts.detail:
```
let target = { a: 1 };
Object.assign(target, { b: 2 }, { a: 3 });
// target is { a: 3, b: 2 }
```
Because it writes into the target, the classic safe-merge idiom passes a fresh literal: `let merged = Object.assign({}, defaults, options);` — the empty literal absorbs everything and `defaults` stays untouched.

```
let o = { x: 1, y: 2 };
let copy = { ...o, z: 3 }; // { x: 1, y: 2, z: 3 }
copy.x = 99;
o.x // still 1
```
Spread may appear anywhere in the literal, and explicit properties written **after** it override the spread values; properties written before are overridden by the spread.

```
let original = { home: { city: "Eugene" } };
let copy = { ...original };
copy.home.city = "Portland";
original.home.city // "Portland" — one home object, two parents
```
Serialization is one way out: `JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(o))` deep-copies anything that survives JSON — at the price of the drops listed next.

```
class Temperature {
  constructor(c) { this.celsius = c; }
  toJSON() { return this.celsius; }
}
JSON.stringify(new Temperature(25)) // "25"
JSON.parse("25")                     // 25
```
`toJSON` is called **before** the replacer and before property enumeration — the earliest hook in the pipeline, and the most powerful.

steps.text:
```
let defaults = { width: 100, height: 100, color: "red" };
let options = { width: 200 };
```

```
let lecture = {
  title: "Closures",
  room: null,
  when: new Date(Date.UTC(2027, 2, 14)),
  canceled: undefined,
  describe() { return `Lecture on ${this.title}`; }
};
