This removes the element **and closes the gap**: `b` is `["a", "c", "d"]` with length 3 — the later elements shift down one index.

concepts.detail:
```
let sum = 0;
for (let v of [1, 2, 3]) {
  if (v < 0) break;
  sum += v;
}
// sum is 6
```
Need the index too? `for (let [i, v] of a.entries())` pairs them up without managing a counter.

```
["a", "b"].forEach((v, i, a) => {
  console.log(i, v, a.length); // 0 a 2, then 1 b 2
});
```
The three-argument signature is worth memorizing — index and whole array arrive free with every value.

steps.text:
```
let sum1 = 0;
for (let v of [10, 20, 30]) {
  sum1 += v;
}
```
No counter, no `.length`, no off-by-one — the iterator hands over 10, 20, 30 and the sum is 60. With `if (v > 25) break;` inserted after the addition, the loop would still have already added 30 — break stops future iterations, not the current one.

```
let sum2 = 0;
[10, 20, 30].forEach(v => { sum2 += v; });
```
The arrow function runs three times with values 10, 20, 30 — sum2 is 60. There is no legal `break` here: `return` only skips the rest of this callback invocation.

```
let sum3 = 0;
let a = [10, 20, 30];
for (let i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
  sum3 += a[i];
}
```
The index `i` runs 0, 1, 2 — you manage it, you may stride it, compare across arrays, or stop on any condition: `if (a[i] > 25) break;` placed *before* the addition here genuinely leaves 30 out.

```
