concepts.detail:
```
"use strict";
function f() { return this; }
f(); // undefined
```
Most modern code (modules, class bodies, transpiled output) is strict, so plan on `undefined` — and on the TypeError the moment a function written as a method is called bare (worked below).

```
let o = {
  value: 42,
  m() { return this.value; }
};
o.m(); // 42 — this is o
```
The binding happens per call: assign the same function to two objects and each call sees its own receiver — `this` is a parameter the invocation passes.

```
let counter = {
  value: 0,
  add(n) { this.value += n; return this; }
};
counter.add(2).add(3).value; // 5
```

steps.text:
```
"use strict";
function whereAmI() {
  return this;
}
```

```
