concepts.detail:
```
function max(first = -Infinity, ...rest) {
  let m = first;
  for (let v of rest) if (v > m) m = v;
  return m;
}
max(3, 1, 4, 1, 5); // 5
```
At the call site, spread performs the inverse: `max(...values)` expands an array into arguments — the two dots are doing mirror-image work (spread out, rest in).

```
function legacy() {
  return arguments.length;
}
legacy(1, 2, 3); // 3 — counting without arrays
```
In strict mode the aliasing disappears (it is a plain snapshot), and in modern code rest parameters retire the whole mechanism.

```
function vectorAdd({ x: x1, y: y1 }, { x: x2, y: y2 }) {
  return { x: x1 + x2, y: y1 + y2 };
}
vectorAdd({ x: 1, y: 2 }, { x: 3, y: 4 }); // { x: 4, y: 6 }
```
Losing the argument loses the binding: `vectorAdd()` throws TypeError reading `x` of undefined — hence the `= {}` default in the worked example.

```
// one name, several shapes:
function greet(name, punctuation = "!") {
  if (typeof name !== "string") name = "stranger";
  return `Hello, ${name}${punctuation}`;
}
```
The parameters themselves become the overloaded signature — the technique behind the flexible APIs of the standard library (`Array.from`, `splice`, `copyWithin`).

steps.text:
```
let seq = 0;
function tag(name = `item-${++seq}`) {
  return name;
}
```

```
function maxRest(first = -Infinity, ...rest) {
  let m = first;
  for (let v of rest) if (v > m) m = v;
  return m;
}
```
At the call, `first` binds 3 and `rest` collects `[1, 4, 1, 5]` — a **true array**, so `for/of` works, as would `.reduce`.

```
function maxLegacy() {
  let m = -Infinity;
  for (let i = 0; i < arguments.length; i++) {
    if (arguments[i] > m) m = arguments[i];
  }
  return m;
}
```
No parameters at all — `arguments` indexes everything: 3, 1, 4, 1, 5 in a manual `i` loop, because `arguments` is array-like, not iterable, and `.forEach` does not exist on it.

```
function area({ width = 10, height = 5 } = {}) {
  return width * height;
}
```
Two defaults in one parameter: `= {}` for the whole argument, and inner defaults for each property the pattern reads.

concepts.detail:
```
function repeat(n, action) { // action is a function argument
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) action(i);
}
repeat(3, i => console.log(`tick ${i}`));
```
The array methods of Chapter 7 are higher-order functions by design — their entire job is "run your callback over my data".

```
const operators = {
  add: (x, y) => x + y,
  subtract: (x, y) => x - y,
  multiply: (x, y) => x * y,
  divide: (x, y) => (y === 0 ? NaN : x / y)
};
operators["multiply"](3, 4); // 12 — computed dispatch
```
Array-valued tables work identically: `handlers[eventType]` is the spine of every event system (Chapter 15).

```
(function () {
  // chunk of code: locals live and die here
  let temp = 40 + 2;
  console.log(temp);
}()); // defined and invoked in one expression
```
The wrapping parentheses matter: without them `function` at statement position parses as a **declaration**, which cannot be immediately invoked — a syntax error. The parens force expression position; the trailing `()` performs the call.

```
const stats = (function () {
  let calls = 0; // private
  function mean(a) {
    calls++;
    return a.reduce((x, y) => x + y) / a.length;
  }
  return { mean, usage: () => calls };
})();
stats.mean([1, 2, 3]); // 2
stats.usage();          // 1 — the count survived the call
```
One global (`stats`), any number of private helpers — the whole trick of the pre-module web.

steps.text:
```
const operators = {
  add: (x, y) => x + y,
  subtract: (x, y) => x - y,
  multiply: (x, y) => x * y,
  divide: (x, y) => (y === 0 ? NaN : x / y)
};
function compute(op, lhs, rhs) {
  let fn = operators[op];
  if (typeof fn === "function") return fn(lhs, rhs);
  throw new TypeError(`unknown operator: ${op}`);
}
```

```
let temp = "global data";
(function () {
  let temp = 40 + 2; // local to the IIFE
  console.log(temp); // 42
})();
console.log(temp); // "global data" — untouched
```
Both variables are named `temp`; only the global one survives the expression.

```
const dice = (function () {
  let count = 0;
  return {
    roll() { count++; return 1 + Math.floor(Math.random() * 6); },
    rolls: () => count
  };
})();
```
The IIFE returns an object; `count` is visible to `roll` and `rolls` (closures — next section) and to nobody else. One global name: `dice`.

concepts.detail:
```
let scope = "global scope";
function checkscope() {
  let scope = "local scope";
  function f() { return scope; }
  return f;
}
checkscope()(); // "local scope" — f was defined inside checkscope
```
The last call happens at global scope; `f` still sees its **defining** scope’s `scope`.

```
function makeCounter() {
  let n = 0; // one variable, captured by reference
  return {
    count: () => ++n,
    reset: () => (n = 0)
  };
}
```
`count` and `reset` operate on the **same** `n`; two calls of `makeCounter()` produce two independent `n`s — the worked example traces both facts.

```
function counter(n = 0) {
  return {
    get count() { return n++; },
    set count(m) {
      if (m > n) n = m;
      else throw new Error("count can only be set to a larger value");
    }
  };
}
```
The parameter itself is the private variable — validated, invisible, and shared by both accessors.

```
let fs = [];
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) fs[i] = () => i;
fs.map(f => f()); // [3, 3, 3] — one shared var

let gs = [];
for (let j = 0; j < 3; j++) gs[j] = () => j;
gs.map(f => f()); // [0, 1, 2] — a fresh let per iteration
```
"Captured by reference" cuts both ways: shared state when you want a counter, wrong state when you wanted snapshots.

```
function memoize(f) {
  const cache = new Map();
  return function (...args) {
    let key = JSON.stringify(args);
    if (cache.has(key)) return cache.get(key);
    let result = f.apply(this, args);
    cache.set(key, result);
    return result;
  };
}
```
The technique pays off only for pure functions — side effects and call-dependent state do not survive being skipped.

steps.text:
```
let scope = "global scope";
function checkscope() {
  let scope = "local scope";   // environment E1
  function f() { return scope; } // f closes over E1
  return f;
}
```
Global environment holds `scope` ("global scope"); the call `checkscope()` creates environment E1 with its own `scope` and, inside it, the function `f`.

```
const uniqueInteger = (function () {
  let counter = 0; // private, permanent
  return function () { return counter++; };
})();
```
Read it in two beats: the outer function **ran immediately** (the trailing `()`), returning the inner function, which was assigned to `uniqueInteger`. Its `counter` lives on in the captured environment — one copy, forever.

```
function counter() {
  let n = 0;
  return {
    count() { return n++; },
    reset() { n = 0; }
  };
}
let c = counter(); // environment E1: { n: 0 }
let d = counter(); // environment E2: { n: 0 }
```
E1 belongs to `c`’s methods, E2 to `d`’s. Different invocations never share bindings — the spelling `n` is identical, the variables are not.

```
c  ---> [ count, reset ] --> environment E1: { n }
d  ---> [ count, reset ] --> environment E2: { n }
```
Each product points its methods at one environment, and the environments point at nothing but their own `n`. Concretely, E1 ran 0 → 1 → (reset to 0) → 1; E2 ran 0 → 1 → 2 — the resets and increments of one counter are invisible to the other, always.

```
const fib = memoize(function (n) {
  return n < 2 ? n : fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
});
```
The recursion calls `fib` — the const binding — so **every recursive call goes through the cache-checking wrapper**. The cache closure is created once, when `memoize` runs.

