concepts.detail:
```
function f(a, b = 1, ...rest) {}
f.length; // 1 — b has a default, rest is rest
function g(a, b) {}
g.length; // 2
```

```
function declared() {}
const anonymous = function () {};
const arrow = x => x;
declared.name;   // "declared"
anonymous.name; // "anonymous" — inferred from the binding
arrow.name;     // "arrow"
({ m() {} }).m.name; // "m"
```
The property is metadata for humans and tools — assigning to it changes nothing about how the function can be called.

```
function Range(from, to) { this.from = from; this.to = to; }
Range.prototype.includes = function (x) {
  return this.from <= x && x <= this.to;
};
new Range(1, 3).includes(2); // true — method found via the prototype
```
Arrow functions cannot be constructors and carry no `prototype` — one more reason arrows and `new` never mix.

```
let scope = "local";
const f = new Function("return scope;");
f(); // ReferenceError — no closure over your scope
```
Historically it served template compilers and runtime evaluation; today `Function` constructors appear in XSS write-ups more than in programs. Prefer closures — which capture — over string evaluation, which cannot.

steps.text:
```
function f(a, b = 1, ...rest) { return a + b; }
const g = function () {};
const h = (x, y) => x + y;
const k = f.bind(null);
```

```
let local = "visible";
const evil = new Function("return local;");
evil(); // ReferenceError: local is not defined
```
The `Function` constructor compiles in **global scope**: no closure over `local`, no matter where the construction line sits. Between that and string-injection risk, the constructor earns its "avoid" label — closures (Section 8.5) capture; string compilation cannot.

concepts.detail:
```
// average as one reduce:
const mean = a => a.reduce((s, v) => s + v, 0) / a.length;
// grouping into a Map:
const groupBy = (a, keyFn) =>
  a.reduce((m, v) => (m.set(keyFn(v), v), m), new Map());
```
The grouping pattern — "return the accumulator itself after each step" — is the idiom to internalize.

```
const compose = (f, g) => (x) => f(g(x));
const square = (x) => x * x;
const halve = (x) => x / 2;
const halfOfSquare = compose(halve, square); // halve(square(x))
halfOfSquare(6); // 18
```
The returned arrow is a closure over `f` and `g` — composition is a direct application of Section 8.5.

steps.text:
```
let s = Math.sqrt(
  data.map(x => x - mean).map(x => x * x).reduce(sum) / (data.length - 1)
);
```
The nested form shows the shape of the computation — transform, transform, collapse — which is the readable skeleton the book uses whenever it analyzes data.

```
const square = (x) => x * x;
const halve = (x) => x / 2;
```

```
const pureArea = (r) => Math.PI * r * r;
let calls = 0;
const loudArea = (r) => { calls++; return pureArea(r); };
```
`pureArea(2)` is `12.566...` on every call forever; `loudArea` additionally increments an external counter — its side effect is part of its contract.

concepts.detail:
```js
// A factory function that returns a new range object.
function range(from, to) {
  const r = Object.create(range.methods);  // r delegates to range.methods
  r.from = from;                            // own data properties
  r.to = to;
  return r;
}

range.methods = {                           // the shared prototype
  includes(x) { return this.from <= x && x <= this.to; },
  toString() { return "(" + this.from + "..." + this.to + ")"; },
};

const r = range(1, 3);
r.includes(2);   // true — method found on the prototype, this = r
```
`Object.create(p)` returns a fresh empty object whose prototype link points at p — the whole wiring in one call.

examples.problem:
Add iteration to the factory-built range so that `[...range(1, 4)]` yields `[1, 2, 3, 4]`, using what the chain already provides:
```js
range.methods[Symbol.iterator] = function* () {
  for (let x = Math.ceil(this.from); x <= this.to; x++) yield x;
};

[...range(1, 4)];   // ?
for (const n of range(3, 5)) console.log(n);   // ?
```

concepts.detail:
```js
function Range(from, to) {
  this.from = from;   // initialize the fresh object
  this.to = to;
}
Range.prototype = {  // methods for instances (see below)
  includes(x) { return this.from <= x && x <= this.to; },
  toString() { return "(" + this.from + "..." + this.to + ")"; },
};

const r = new Range(1, 3);   // r inherits Range.prototype
```
The classic bug of this era: calling `Range(1, 3)` **without** new runs the same body with `this` bound to the global object (or undefined in strict mode, Section 5.5) — silently writing globals. Classes (Section 9.3) close this hole by throwing when called without new.

```js
new Range(1, 3)
// 1. const o = {};                                  create
// 2. Object.setPrototypeOf(o, Range.prototype);      link
// 3. const result = Range.call(o, 1, 3);             initialize (this = o)
// 4. return (result is an object) ? result : o;     return
```
Step 2 is the inheritance: step 3 merely fills in data. Keep the four steps memorized — they are the definition of everything `class` (9.3) and `extends` (9.5) do.

examples.problem:
Predict each result before running:
```js
function Wizard(power) { this.power = power; }
function Ghost() { return { spooky: true }; }
function Wand(size) { this.size = size; return 42; }

new Wizard(10);            // ?
new Ghost();               // instanceof Ghost → ?
new Wand(9).size;           // ?
```

concepts.detail:
```js
class Range {
  constructor(from, to) {
    this.from = from;
    this.to = to;
  }
  includes(x) { return this.from <= x && x <= this.to; }
  toString() { return "(" + this.from + "..." + this.to + ")"; }
}
```
Two more strictness upgrades: the class body executes in **strict mode** regardless of the file, and calling a class without `new` throws a TypeError — the Section 9.2 forgotten-new bug is now impossible.

```js
typeof Range;                                  // "function"
Object.getOwnPropertyNames(Range.prototype);
// ["constructor", "includes", "toString"]
```
The name list is exactly what the manual pattern builds — the difference is enumerability (class methods are invisible to for/in) and the stricter defaults listed above.

```js
class Range {
  /* ...instance methods as above... */
  static parse(s) {
    const [from, to] = s.split("..").map(Number);
    return new Range(from, to);
  }
}
Range.parse("1..3");     // factory — same class, different entry point
new Range(1, 3).parse;   // undefined — not on instances
```
Built-ins work this way everywhere: `Number.parseInt`, `Object.keys`, `Date.now` (Section 11).

```js
class Temperature {
  constructor(celsius = 0) { this.celsius = celsius; }
  get fahrenheit() { return this.celsius * 9 / 5 + 32; }
  set fahrenheit(f) { this.celsius = (f - 32) * 5 / 9; }
}
const t = new Temperature(0);
t.fahrenheit;        // 32 — computed on read
```
See the worked example: one stored field, one derived view, validated on write.

```js
class Safe {
  #secret = 42;
  reveal() { return this.#secret; }   // fine inside the body
}
new Safe().reveal();   // 42
new Safe().#secret;    // SyntaxError — the name does not exist out here
```
Rules: `#` fields must be **declared** in the body (you cannot invent them ad hoc), every instance gets its own copy, and `static #x` / private accessors `get #x()` exist too. Compare the old convention `_secret` — a warning label, not a lock.

```js
class Config {
  static #env;
  static {
    try { Config.#env = JSON.parse(globalThis.appConfig); }
    catch { Config.#env = {}; }        // sane default on any failure
  }
  static get env() { return Config.#env; }
}
```
One class may have several static blocks; they run in source order interleaved with static field initializers.

examples.problem:
Write Range as a class and as the manual constructor+prototype pattern, then verify they are the same shape:
```js
class Range {
  constructor(from, to) { this.from = from; this.to = to; }
  includes(x) { return this.from <= x && x <= this.to; }
  toString() { return "(" + this.from + "..." + this.to + ")"; }
}

function RangeFn(from, to) { this.from = from; this.to = to; }
RangeFn.prototype.includes = function (x) {
  return this.from <= x && x <= this.to;
};
RangeFn.prototype.toString = function () {
  return "(" + this.from + "..." + this.to + ")";
};

typeof Range;                                    // ?
Object.getOwnPropertyNames(Range.prototype);    // ?
new Range(1, 3) instanceof Range;                // ?
```

Store a temperature once (celsius) and expose fahrenheit as a computed, write-validated property:
```js
class Temperature {
  constructor(celsius = 0) { this.celsius = celsius; }
  get fahrenheit() { return this.celsius * 9 / 5 + 32; }
  set fahrenheit(f) {
    if (f < -459.67) throw new RangeError("below absolute zero");
    this.celsius = (f - 32) * 5 / 9;
  }
  static fromFahrenheit(f) { return new Temperature((f - 32) * 5 / 9); }
}

const t = new Temperature(0);
t.fahrenheit;         // ?
t.fahrenheit = 212;  // ?
t.celsius;           // ?
Temperature.fromFahrenheit(98.6).celsius;   // ?
```

Build a counter whose count cannot be read or corrupted from outside, and count how many counters exist:
```js
class Counter {
  static #created = 0;
  #count;
  constructor(start = 0) {
    this.#count = start;
    Counter.#created++;
  }
  increment(by = 1) { this.#count += by; return this; }
  get value() { return this.#count; }
  static get instances() { return Counter.#created; }
}

const c = new Counter(10);
c.increment(5).increment();
c.value;           // ?
c.count;            // ?
c.#count;         // ?
Counter.instances; // ?
```

concepts.detail:
```js
Object.assign(Range.prototype, {
  equals(r) { return this.from === r.from && this.to === r.to; },
  overlaps(r) { return this.from <= r.to && r.from <= this.to; },
});
new Range(1, 3).equals(new Range(1, 3));   // true
```
This is the civilized descendant of Section 9.2’s wholesale replacement — which severed `constructor` — and the same tool mixins use in Section 9.5.

```js
Object.defineProperty(Array.prototype, "last", {
  value: function () { return this[this.length - 1]; },
  writable: true, configurable: true,
  enumerable: false,   // invisible to for/in — like every built-in method
});
[10, 20, 30].last();   // 30, and loops stay clean
```
Understand it as the explanation for why built-ins never poisoned loops — not as a license.

```js
if (!Array.prototype.flat) {
  Object.defineProperty(Array.prototype, "flat", {
    value: function (depth = 1) { /* spec algorithm */ },
    writable: true, configurable: true, enumerable: false,
  });
}
```
The guard means modern engines never see the patch (no collision with the native `flat`); the descriptor keeps `for/in` clean. Polyfill libraries (core-js) exist precisely so application code never hand-rolls this.

