Span.parse;           // inherited static, callable on the subclass
```
The chain behind s is `s` → `Span.prototype` → `Range.prototype` → `Object.prototype` → null: Section 9.1’s linear search, one link longer.

```js
class TypedMap extends Map {
  #valueType;
  constructor(valueType, entries) {
    validate(entries);        // fine: needs no this
    // this.#valueType = ... // ReferenceError — super has not run
    super(entries);          // object is created HERE
    this.#valueType = valueType;   // now this exists
  }
}
```
The rule exists because base constructors may return exotic objects (Section 9.2’s return rule) — the derived class cannot assume what `this` will be until the base says so.

```js
class LoggedRange extends Range {
  includes(x) {
    console.log("checking", x);
    return super.includes(x);   // borrow, do not reimplement
  }
}
```
`super` here means "start the lookup one level above the prototype this method is defined on" — not "the parent of this instance", a distinction that matters when methods move around (Section 14.6’s proxies can even intercept it).

```js
class EZArray extends Array {
  get first() { return this[0]; }
  get last() { return this[this.length - 1]; }
}
EZArray.of(10, 20, 30).last;      // 30 — a REAL array with extras
EZArray.of(1, 2) instanceof Array; // true

class NotFound extends Error {}   // catch (e) { if (e instanceof NotFound) }
```
What `map` and `filter` construct for subclasses is steered by the `Symbol.species` machinery (Section 14.3) — by default, the subclass itself.

```js
class AbstractSet {
  constructor() {
    if (new.target === AbstractSet) {
      throw new TypeError("AbstractSet is abstract — subclass it");
    }
  }
  has(x) { throw new Error("subclass must implement has()"); }
}
class SingletonSet extends AbstractSet {
  constructor(member) { super(); this.member = member; }
  has(x) { return x === this.member; }
}
```
The unimplemented `has` doubling as a stub turns forgetting into a loud failure instead of silent inheritance of nonsense.

```js
const Serialization = {
  serialize() { return JSON.stringify(this); },
};
class Note {}
Object.assign(Note.prototype, Serialization);
```
One caveat: `Object.assign` copies **values**, invoking any getters on the source (Section 6.6) — accessor-preserving mixins need `Object.defineProperties(target, Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptors(source))`. Mixins can also be written as factories that return subclasses when true composition-with-state is needed.

