concepts.detail:
In ES6 a class body is itself a namespace: only the class binding is global; its static methods live behind it:
```js
const Stats = {
  mean(nums) { return nums.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / nums.length; },
  stddev(nums) { /* ... */ }
};

Stats.mean([2, 4, 6]);   // 4

class Convert {
  static toCelsius(f) { return (f - 32) * 5 / 9; }
}

Convert.toCelsius(212);  // 100
```
This hides nothing — the functions are still reachable — but it organizes the global namespace, which is half the battle.

