Wrapping events in a promise is Section 13.2’s promisify pattern — it lets the handler below stay a flat async function. Add a size cap before concat: an unbounded body is an unbounded Buffer (memory as a denial-of-service).

```js
const res = await fetch("https://api.example.com/users/7");
if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
const user = await res.json();   // second await — body, not headers
```
POSTing JSON: `fetch(url, { method: "POST", headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" }, body: JSON.stringify(data) })` — exactly the browser spelling, so client code is portable between hosts.

```js
const ac = new AbortController();
const timer = setTimeout(() => ac.abort(), 5000);
try {
  const res = await fetch(url, { signal: ac.signal });
  return await res.json();
} finally {
  clearTimeout(timer);
}
```
Five seconds of silence, and the promise rejects instead of the caller waiting forever — timeouts belong to the caller’s policy, not the network’s mood.

examples.problem:
Build a framework-free JSON API server: `GET /api/time` returns the current time; `POST /api/echo` reads the JSON request body and echoes it back with a byte count; anything else gets a 404 — all with correct headers and async/await throughout.
```js
const http = require("node:http");

// req is a Readable stream — collect its body (Section 16.3 + 13.2)
function readBody(req) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const chunks = [];
    req.on("data", (c) => chunks.push(c));
    req.on("end", () => resolve(Buffer.concat(chunks).toString("utf8")));
    req.on("error", reject);
  });
}

const server = http.createServer(async (req, res) => {
  const { pathname } = new URL(req.url, "http://localhost");

  if (req.method === "GET" && pathname === "/api/time") {
    const body = JSON.stringify({ iso: new Date().toISOString() });
    res.writeHead(200, { "Content-Type": "application/json" });
    res.end(body);
    return;
  }

  if (req.method === "POST" && pathname === "/api/echo") {
    const text = await readBody(req);
    let json = null;
    try { json = JSON.parse(text); } catch { /* fall through to 400 */ }
    if (json === null) {
      res.writeHead(400, { "Content-Type": "application/json" });
      res.end(JSON.stringify({ error: "body is not valid JSON" }));
      return;
    }
    const body = JSON.stringify({ youSent: json, bytes: Buffer.byteLength(text) });
    res.writeHead(200, { "Content-Type": "application/json" });
    res.end(body);
    return;
  }

  res.writeHead(404, { "Content-Type": "application/json" });
  res.end(JSON.stringify({ error: `no route: ${req.method} ${pathname}` }));
});

server.listen(3000, () => console.log("JSON API on http://localhost:3000"));
```

