concepts.detail:
```js
const net = require("node:net");
net.createServer((conn) => {
  conn.on("data", (chunk) => conn.write(chunk));  // echo
  conn.on("end", () => console.log("client left"));
}).listen(5900);
```
`conn` also carries `remoteAddress`/`remotePort`, and errors arrive as events — destroy on error, or the socket leaks.

```js
const { exec } = require("node:child_process");
exec("ls -1 src", (err, stdout, stderr) => {
  if (err) return console.error(err);
  console.log(stdout.split("\n").filter(Boolean).length);
});
```
Shell injection in one line: `exec("echo " + userInput)` — userInput `"; rm -rf /"` is a command. With untrusted input, use spawn with an argument array (no shell, no metacharacters).

```js
const { spawn } = require("node:child_process");
const child = spawn("grep", ["-c", "TODO", "big.txt"]);  // args verbatim
let n = "";
child.stdout.on("data", (c) => (n += c));
child.on("close", (code) => console.log(`grep exited ${code}: ${n.trim()} hits`));
```
Live output, unbounded size, and `child.stdin` writable for feeding the child — streaming both ways where exec would have buffered it all.

```js
// the race-free increment:
const cell = new Int32Array(new SharedArrayBuffer(4));
Atomics.add(cell, 0, 1);   // like cell[0]++ — but never interleaved
```
`cell[0]++` is three steps — load, add, store — and two threads doing it concurrently can both read the old value and lose an increment: the exact race OSC Chapter 6 opens with. Atomics is that chapter’s hardware instructions (test-and-set, compare-and-swap) surfaced in JavaScript — reach for message passing first, and Atomics when a shared counter is genuinely the design.

