concepts.detail:
```js
// @flow
function tag(width: number, label: string): string {
  return `${label}:${width}`;
}
tag(3, "x");     // fine
tag("3", "x");   // error: string is not a number
tag(3);          // error: missing argument — arity is typed too
```
The errors are **before execution** — the entire point: move the `tag("3", ...)` failure from a user’s console to the author’s editor.

```js
// @flow
function area(width: number, height: ?number): number {
  const h = height ?? 1;   // refinement: h is now plain number
  return width * h;        // without the ??, this line is an error
}
```
This is the billion-dollar bug (Tony Hoare’s “null references … my billion-dollar mistake”) turned into a type-system game: absence is representable, and only explicitly-checked absence is usable. It is exactly the discipline `?.` and `??` (Sections 3.4, 17.4) enforce at runtime, moved to compile time.

```js
// @flow
type Result =
  | { ok: true, value: string }
  | { ok: false, error: Error };

function render(r: Result): string {
  if (r.ok) {
    return r.value;        // narrowed: only this variant has .value
  }
  return r.error.message;   // narrowed the other way
}
```
Unions model every “one of several shapes” domain — API responses, parsed results, state machines — and the discriminant pattern makes illegal combinations unrepresentable.

