concepts.statement:
**Write the primary template as the default case; write partial specializations as pattern-matched cases.** The compiler chooses the most specialized match — the pattern matching IS the program:
```
template<typename T> struct Remove_ref { using type = T; };
template<typename T> struct Remove_ref<T&>  { using type = T; };
template<typename T> struct Remove_ref<T&&> { using type = T; };
```

**To choose a type by a compile-time condition, specialize on the condition**
```
template<bool B, typename T, typename F> struct Select { using type = T; };
template<typename T, typename F> struct Select<false, T, F> { using type = F; };
```
or use `std::conditional<B,T,F>::type`, or — inside a function — `if constexpr (B) ... else ...`, which discards the untaken branch entirely.

