concepts.statement:
**Head plus tail, with the tail as base class**
```
template<typename... Ts> struct Tuple;              // primary declaration

template<typename T, typename... Rest>
struct Tuple<T, Rest...> : Tuple<Rest...> {         // a head plus the tail
    T head;
    constexpr Tuple(const T& t, const Rest&... rest)
        : Tuple<Rest...>(rest...), head{t} { }
};

template<> struct Tuple<> { };                      // the empty tuple: base case
```

**To reach element N, cast down to the N-th base and take its head**
```
template<unsigned N>
struct Get {                                        // the descent
    template<typename T, typename... Rest>
    static constexpr auto& get(Tuple<T, Rest...>& t)
    { return Get<N-1>::get(static_cast<Tuple<Rest...>&>(t)); }
};

template<>
struct Get<0> {                                    // the head
    template<typename T, typename... Rest>
    static constexpr T& get(Tuple<T, Rest...>& t) { return t.head; }
};
```

**A wrapper takes the pack by forwarding reference and re-expands it through `std::forward`**
```
template<typename C, typename... Args>
void emplace_back(C& c, Args&&... args)
{
    c.emplace_back(std::forward<Args>(args)...);
}
```
Each argument keeps its value category and exact type across the boundary.

**Dimensions become template arguments**
```
template<int M, int K, int S>
struct Unit {                     // a unit with dimensions M, K, S
    static constexpr int m  = M;  // exponent of meters
    static constexpr int kg = K;  // exponent of kilograms
    static constexpr int s  = S;  // exponent of seconds
};

template<typename U>
struct Quantity {                 // a magnitude in unit U
    double v;
    constexpr explicit Quantity(double d) : v{d} { }
};
```

**The signature IS the rule**
```
template<int M, int K, int S>
constexpr Quantity<Unit<M,K,S>>
operator+(Quantity<Unit<M,K,S>> a, Quantity<Unit<M,K,S>> b)
{
    return Quantity<Unit<M,K,S>>{a.v + b.v};
}
```
Both arguments must instantiate the SAME unit type — otherwise no `operator+` exists at all.

**Products combine dimensions the way physics does — by adding exponents**
```
template<int M1,int K1,int S1, int M2,int K2,int S2>
constexpr Quantity<Unit<M1+M2, K1+K2, S1+S2>>
operator*(Quantity<Unit<M1,K1,S1>> a, Quantity<Unit<M2,K2,S2>> b)
{
    return Quantity<Unit<M1+M2, K1+K2, S1+S2>>{a.v * b.v};
}
```
Division (written the same way with subtractions) cancels units symmetrically.

**The 2-D product is the classical triple loop, guarded by an extents check**
```
template<typename T>
Matrix<T,2> operator*(const Matrix<T,2>& a, const Matrix<T,2>& b)
{
    Matrix<T,2> res(a.extent(0), b.extent(1));   // an m×n times n×p is m×p
    for (int i = 0; i != a.extent(0); ++i)
        for (int j = 0; j != b.extent(1); ++j)
            for (int k = 0; k != a.extent(1); ++k)
                res(i,j) += a(i,k) * b(k,j);
    return res;
}
```
`assert(a.extent(1) == b.extent(0))` fires when the shapes do not chain.

**The descriptor every matrix and view carries**
```
template<size_t N>
struct Matrix_slice {
    size_t start;                  // offset of the first element
    array<size_t, N> extents;       // number of elements per dimension
    array<size_t, N> strides;       // offset step along each dimension
    // ... plus size(), and the offset computation
};
```

**Given an n×n `Matrix<double,2>` A and a right-hand side `Vector b`, find the `Vector x` with A·x = b — where Vector is simply a 1-D matrix:**
```
using Vector = Matrix<double,1>;

Vector classical_gaussian_elimination(Matrix<double,2> A, Vector b);
Vector pivotal_elimination(Matrix<double,2> A, Vector b);
```

