concepts.statement:
**Resource Acquisition Is Initialization**: acquire the resource in a constructor and release it in the destructor of an object placed in the scope where the resource is needed. Because C++ destroys automatic objects deterministically when a scope exits — normally or via an exception — the destructor, and therefore the release, **must run**:
```
void use() {
    vector<int> v(1000);   // acquire: v owns its buffer
    // ... work, even throwing work ...
}                        // release guaranteed here
```
`vector`, `fstream`, `lock_guard` and the smart pointers of §34.3 are all RAII handles.

**`weak_ptr<T>`** observes an object owned by `shared_ptr`s without owning it: it points at the control block but never keeps the strong count nonzero. If the object dies, the `weak_ptr` **expires** — test with `expired()` or promote safely with `lock()`, which returns a `shared_ptr` (null if the object is gone):
```
struct Node { weak_ptr<Node> next; };
if (auto n = node.next.lock()) { /* safe: n keeps it alive */ }
```
Weak references are the standard cure for the two structures plain shared ownership cannot express: **cycles** and **caches**.

`allocator<T>` offers exactly the raw layer:
```
allocator<double> a;
double* p = a.allocate(4);          // room for 4 doubles; nothing constructed
for (int i = 0; i < 4; ++i) a.construct(p + i, 1.0 / (i + 1));
for (int i = 0; i < 4; ++i) a.destroy(p + i);
a.deallocate(p, 4);                 // n must match the allocate call
```
The pairing rules are strict: never deallocate what you did not allocate, never destroy what you did not construct — and never *use* what you did not construct.

`uninitialized_copy(first, last, out)` copies the range `[first, last)` into *raw* memory at `out`, constructing as it goes; `uninitialized_fill(first, last, v)` constructs copies of `v` over the whole raw range; `uninitialized_fill_n` is the count form. They are the bridge between an allocator's bytes and real objects:
```
auto q = a.allocate(n);
uninitialized_fill(q, q + n, 0.0);   // n doubles, all constructed
for (auto e = q; e < q + n; ++e) a.destroy(e);
a.deallocate(q, n);
```
`vector`'s growth is precisely an `uninitialized_copy` of the old elements into a larger buffer.

Time anything with the same four lines:
```
auto t0 = steady_clock::now();
do_work();
auto t1 = steady_clock::now();
auto d = duration_cast<milliseconds>(t1 - t0);
cout << d.count() << " ms\n";
```
Subtract the points (a duration in the clock's native unit — often nanoseconds), cast to the unit you want, read `.count()` for the raw number. `sleep_for` and the timed waits take durations directly, so units are checked by the type system.

**`regex`** — `basic_regex<char>` — *compiles* its pattern the moment it is constructed: the object holds the compiled state machine, not the text. Construction is comparatively expensive (parsing, allocation, table building), so build once and reuse; a malformed pattern throws **`regex_error`** carrying what went wrong. Constructor flags tune the machine: `regex::icase` folds case, `optimize` spends construction time to speed matching, `nosubs` drops capture bookkeeping.
```
regex pat {R"(\d{5}(-\d{4})?)"};   // compile once
smatch m;
bool ok = regex_match("02158-6543", m, pat);
```

An **`sregex_iterator`** ranges over all non-overlapping matches: construct the begin from an input range and a pattern; the default-constructed iterator is the end sentinel; each `*it` is a full `smatch`. It is `regex_search` in increment form — each advance searches from the end of the previous match:
```
for (auto it = sregex_iterator(text.begin(), text.end(), num);
     it != sregex_iterator(); ++it)
    cout << it->str() << " at " << it->position() << "\n";
```

